Found during the #9 arch audit.

.github/workflows/docker-publish.yml builds with `--platform linux/amd64` at both call sites (PR build line 71, push line 83). The OCP CLI runs outside the cluster so an amd64-only CLI image is survivable, but robocop runs IN the cluster, i.e. on the target architecture. On an arm64 cluster the robocop pod would fail to pull/exec.

Both Dockerfiles are now arch-portable (Dockerfile kubectl download fixed in #9; Dockerfile.robocop had no arch assumptions and its base perl:5.40-slim is multi-arch), so the only remaining blocker is the publish step.

Fix is one line -> `--platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64`, BUT it has a real cost that needs a maintainer decision: the Dockerfile compiles Perl 5.42 from source, and an arm64 leg on a QEMU-emulated amd64 runner will take a very long time. Options: native arm64 runner, or split the matrix into two native jobs plus a manifest merge.

Second, separate inconsistency found in the same place: nothing builds or publishes the images the manifests actually reference.
- manifests/robocop/deployment.yaml + share/robocop/deployment.yaml -> ghcr.io/getty/ocp:latest
- manifests/robocop-deployment.yaml -> ghcr.io/getty/ocp-robocop:latest
- manifests/robocop/overlays/dev/kustomization.yaml -> ghcr.io/getty/ocp
The only workflow pushes raudssus/ocp to Docker Hub. Verified: ghcr.io/getty/ocp and ghcr.io/getty/ocp-robocop do not exist (registry token 403 DENIED, github package page 404). Decide on one registry and make the manifests and the workflow agree.
